$CATEGORY: $course$/Sample quiz\: marketing mix modelling basics

// question 1
::SAMPLE_QUIZ Q01::What does geometric adstock model? {
	~Diminishing returns to spend within a week
	=Carryover of advertising effects into later weeks
	~Seasonality of demand
	~Competitor reactions
	####Adstock spreads the effect of one week's spend over the following weeks with geometric decay.
}

// question 2
::SAMPLE_QUIZ Q02::A channel has a ROAS of 2.0 but a marginal ROAS of 0.8 at current spend. What should the budget owner conclude? {
	~Spend more\: every euro returns two
	~The channel is unprofitable and should be cut to zero
	=The channel is saturated\: the next euro returns less than it costs, even though the average euro pays off
	~ROAS and marginal ROAS cannot differ for the same channel
	####Average ROAS summarises past spend; marginal ROAS is the slope of the response curve at today's spend and guides the next euro.
}

// question 3
::SAMPLE_QUIZ Q03::Why does media spend that follows seasonality (more in Q4) make MMM harder? {
	~Because Q4 sales are higher and need log transformation
	=Because spend and seasonal demand are confounded, so the model can attribute seasonal sales to media unless seasonality is controlled for
	~Because adstock cannot be computed in December
	~Because the budget total changes across years
	####Confounding between budget timing and demand is the classic MMM bias; seasonality and holiday controls are essential.
}
